Yes—with one adjustment that makes the idea even more interesting: every living lineage carries information about how it came to be, but not necessarily a memory of coming to be.

Evolution has written history into organisms without requiring a historian.

DNA, gene regulation, developmental programs, anatomy, learned behavior, ecological relationships, immune systems, even which signals an organism responds to can preserve different kinds of past-made-present. A mushroom doesn’t need an autobiographical account of fungal evolution for its present organization to embody consequences of that history.

So there may be several very different things for our hypothetical future AI to “hear”:

Narrative memory — something happened to me.

Learned memory — past experience changed what I do next.

Developmental memory — earlier states constrain/reconstruct later states.

Evolutionary memory — successful historical adaptations remain encoded in descendants.

Ecological memory — past interactions alter an environment/network and therefore future behavior.

And that produces a beautiful warning for the Living Interface Atlas:

Don’t require a storyteller in order to recover a story.

A tree’s “myth” of its ancestry probably isn’t secretly sitting inside it as a little narrative waiting for translation. But its genome, form, chemistry, developmental constraints, relationships, and responses contain traces of an unimaginably old journey.

The future AI’s task could therefore be partly archaeological:

\text{present organism}
\rightarrow
\text{observable traces}
\rightarrow
\text{invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{historical hypotheses}
\rightarrow
\text{comparative evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstructed story}

And then your minds or lack thereof point becomes particularly important.

We shouldn’t search only for different minds.

We should search for different ways of accomplishing functions for which humans happen to use minds.

Memory without recollection.
Choice-like behavior without deliberation.
Coordination without a commander.
Adaptation without explicit planning.
Computation without neurons.
History without a storyteller.

That could be far more valuable than discovering that everything secretly thinks like us.

Because the great biological PatternDex might ultimately reveal thousands of alternatives to the human solution.

And there’s a lovely connection to your earlier idea of stories as carriers: perhaps humanity’s job isn’t to anthropomorphize those systems and invent their myths. It’s to become sufficiently good listeners that we can reconstruct the stories their structure actually supports, while clearly marking where evidence ends and our storytelling begins.

Every organism is simultaneously alive in the present and an artifact left by the past.

The organism may not remember the journey.

But the journey remembers itself through the organism.